# An alias split across caption tokens still matches — Plan

Implements **OD-6** (`docs/DESIGN.oracle.md`), which adds **R1002** from the
evidence in **BL-8**: `normalize` joins only letter-to-digit transitions, never
two multi-letter fragments, so a caption that renders `Tomahawk` as `toma hawk`
matches nothing at all. Of 52 mangled variants tested against the shipped table,
three failed — `toma hawk`, `aor us master`, `air us elite` — and each failure
is a video dropping silently out of the corpus the report is built from.

## Summary

Match an alias against *whole tokens* rather than against a literal string: an
alias also matches where its normalized text is the concatenation of adjacent
tokens in the text, with every space inside the alias landing on a token
boundary. `toma hawk` then matches `tomahawk`, and `theb650` still cannot match
`b650`, because the match must begin at a token start and end at a token end.

- **The mechanism is one regex, still.** Each surface form compiles with an
  optional single space between adjacent characters and a mandatory single space
  where the form itself has one. This keeps `compile_matcher`'s one-pattern scan
  (a two-hour transcript is read once), keeps longest-form-first precedence, and
  keeps `matched_form` reporting the text that actually matched — so
  `aliases --check` names `toma hawk` as the spelling that did the work. The
  signatures do not change.
- **Joining is permitted across one space and nothing else.** After
  normalization a space is the only separator a caption leaves between fragments,
  so a slash or an ampersand keeps two tokens apart. This is the line between
  "captions split words" and "un-anchoring the matcher".
- **`normalize` folds the hyphen to a space** (R1002 says so), which retires the
  rule that kept it as a character. `x670e-plus` normalizes to `x670e plus` and
  is matched by a table form spelled either way; `steel-legend` folds onto the
  table's `steel legend`. **Two existing tests assert the old behaviour and are
  rewritten** — visible under `Blind-Tests`, and the reason is R1002.
- **The table gains the mishearings a join cannot recover** — `air us elite`,
  `air us master`. R1002 leaves those to the table because they are data (§9),
  not a rule.
- **The 52-variant set is reconstructed, not recovered.** BL-8's measurement was
  taken offline and never landed in the tree, so the fixture is rebuilt to the
  same size and classes, and the three named failures are in it by name. It
  pins recall as a suite-level number, not as prose.
- **Not done here: the ITX chipset variant (OD-7/R1003).** It is a different
  decision with its own plan, and it edits the same two modules — so the two
  plans are built one after the other, not in parallel.
- **Not done here: matching across a cue boundary.** A name split by the cue
  break itself (`…toma` / `hawk…`) stays invisible; R1002 asks for token joining
  within the text it is given, and per-cue matching is R3's existing shape.

Three slices, ~430 lines, **sequential**. Cost to the owner: one behaviour
change to a normalization rule two blind tests currently pin, and a slightly
larger compiled pattern (~2x the alternation text, same single pass).

**What I need you to rule on**, all recorded as uncertainties below and all
proceeded on: (1) that joining spans a single space only; (2) that the
reconstructed variant fixture is an acceptable substitute for BL-8's unrecorded
52; (3) that `air us master` rides along with the `air us elite` BL-8 actually
observed; (4) that the hyphen fold covers the ASCII hyphen alone.

## Uncertainties

Run unattended, so nothing stopped for a ruling: each entry records the default
proceeded on, and each is in the report to the owner.

- **Q:** What counts as "adjacent" for two tokens the join may fuse? R1002 says
  "adjacent whole tokens" without naming the separator. — **risk:** HIGH — it is
  the matcher's contract, and the blind test author asserts against it.
  — **proposed:** exactly one space. After `normalize`, a single space is what a
  caption leaves between the fragments of a split word (and, once the hyphen
  folds, what a hyphen leaves too); every other surviving character — `/`, `&`,
  `+` — is punctuation the normalizer deliberately kept, and a word split across
  one of those is not caption damage.
  **Ruling:** proceeded on the default (HIGH, filed here rather than as a
  `BL-<n>`, because the steward cannot stop for a ruling; the oracle reviews it
  next cycle).
- **Q:** BL-8's 52-variant set is not in the repository — no fixture, no script,
  no journal entry carries it. What lands as "the measured 52-variant set"?
  — **risk:** HIGH — it is the fixture the blind test author writes assertions
  from. — **proposed:** rebuild it: the three named failures verbatim, plus the
  mangling classes the shipped table and `normalize`'s docstring already
  enumerate (spaced part numbers, split digits, hyphenated forms, spelled-out
  vendor initials, plurals, mishearings), to at least 52 rows with the expected
  canonical on each. The reconstruction is stated in the fixture's header so
  nobody later reads it as the original measurement.
  **Ruling:** proceeded on the default (HIGH, as above).
- **Q:** Which mishearings does "the table gains the observed spoken forms"
  cover? BL-8 observed `air us elite`; `air us master` was not named.
  — **risk:** LOW — table data, reversible by editing a file.
  — **proposed:** add `air us elite` and `air us master`. The mishearing is of
  *Aorus*, not of the family suffix, so a table that recovers only the Elite is a
  table that will lose the Master on the next video. A bare `air us` is NOT
  added: with no family word behind it, it matches any sentence about any other
  Aorus board. **Ruling:** proceeded on the default (LOW).
- **Q:** Does "folds hyphens to spaces" include the Unicode dashes?
  — **risk:** LOW — one character class in one function.
  — **proposed:** the ASCII hyphen-minus only. It is what `normalize` names
  today in `_SEPARATORS`, and an en dash in a caption separates clauses rather
  than fragments of a part number. **Ruling:** proceeded on the default (LOW).
- **Q:** Does the literal-string match survive alongside token joining? R1002
  says an alias "also matches" the joined form. — **risk:** LOW — no candidate
  answer changes the code. **proposed:** it survives, and it does so for free:
  a literal occurrence is the case where no inter-token space is deleted, so the
  one pattern covers both and there is no second matcher to keep in step.
  **Ruling:** proceeded on the default (LOW).

## The work, sliced

Three slices, **sequential**, and each is observable on its own: the
normalization fold, then the matching rule, then the table and the recall
fixture that measures both. They are not parallel — slice 2's pattern is built
over the text slice 1 produces, and slice 3 asserts recall against both. The
file lists are disjoint anyway, so a later re-slicing into parallel work costs
no rewriting.

## Slice 1 — A hyphen is a space, so `steel-legend` reaches the table

- **Delivers:** `normalize("The X670E-PLUS board")` is `"the x670e plus board"`,
  and `steel-legend` in a caption normalizes to the table's `steel legend` and is
  counted as a mention of Steel Legend. Observable in `aliases --check`: the
  Steel Legend row gains the hyphenated spelling as a matched form on any video
  that writes it that way. Covers R1002 (its normalization clause).
- **Files:** `src/find_best_mobo/normalize.py`, `tests/test_normalize.py`
- **Estimate:** ~90 lines

### Signatures

```python
def normalize(text: str) -> str: ...
```

Unchanged — this slice changes what it returns, not how it is called. Nothing
else in the module is public.

### Behaviour the signature cannot carry

- **The ASCII hyphen becomes a space before anything else runs**, in the same
  translation pass that drops punctuation — `str.maketrans` maps `-` to `" "`
  rather than to `None`, so `x670e-plus` and `x670e - plus` and `x670e plus` all
  land on one string. Whitespace collapsing then runs as it does today, so a run
  of hyphens does not leave a run of spaces.
- **The joining rule is untouched.** `_SEPARATORS` may drop `-` once hyphens can
  no longer reach it, but `x-670-e` must still fold to `x670e` — it now does so
  as `x 670 e`, through the space branch that already existed. This is a
  deletion, not a new rule, and the existing folding tests are the check.
- **Two tests assert the retired behaviour and are rewritten in this slice**:
  `test_hyphen_survives_as_a_character` and
  `test_hyphenated_model_name_is_left_intact` in `tests/test_normalize.py`. They
  were written blind and were right about the design as it then stood; R1002
  changed the design. The commit that rewrites them says exactly that, per
  `AGENTS.md`'s blind-test rule — it is not a test weakened to fit an
  implementation, and the replacement asserts the new equality rather than
  deleting the case. The module docstring's "the one rule worth stating twice"
  paragraph is rewritten with them.
- **Idempotence and totality survive.** `normalize(normalize(t)) == normalize(t)`
  and it never raises; both are already property-tested and must stay green.
- **`data/aliases.toml` needs no edit for this.** Surface forms pass through the
  same function, so the table's one hyphenated form (`pro-rs`) folds onto its
  own `pro rs` and is de-duplicated by `compile_matcher`'s `seen` set — a
  redundant row, not a broken one. Removing it is slice 3's, with the other
  table work.

## Slice 2 — A name the captions split in two still matches

- **Delivers:** `toma hawk` counts as a mention of MAG Tomahawk and `aor us
  master` as one of Aorus Master, in bodies and in titles alike, while `theb650`
  still matches nothing. Observable in `aliases --check`, where those spellings
  appear in the matched-forms column of their canonicals, and in `select`'s
  in/out counts, which gain the videos those mentions carry over the threshold.
  Covers R1002.
- **Files:** `src/find_best_mobo/aliases.py`, `tests/test_aliases.py`, `docs/architecture.md`
- **Estimate:** ~180 lines

### Signatures

```python
def compile_matcher(aliases: Sequence[Alias]) -> re.Pattern[str]: ...
def find_mentions(transcript: Transcript, matcher: re.Pattern[str]) -> tuple[Mention, ...]: ...
def find_title_hits(video: Video, matcher: re.Pattern[str]) -> frozenset[str]: ...
```

All three keep the signatures they have today, and `Alias` and `Mention` keep
their fields. `Alias` and `Mention` live in `src/find_best_mobo/aliases.py`;
`Transcript` and `Cue` in `src/find_best_mobo/transcripts.py`; `Video` in
`src/find_best_mobo/index.py` — stated per OD-12, so no author has to guess a
module. The change is entirely inside the pattern `compile_matcher` builds.

### Behaviour the signatures cannot carry

- **The token-join rule, stated exactly.** For a normalized surface form `f`,
  the compiled alternative matches a run of adjacent tokens whose concatenation
  is `f`, where each space *inside* `f` must coincide with a token boundary in
  the text. Equivalently, and this is the implementation: between two adjacent
  non-space characters of `f`, the pattern permits an optional single space;
  where `f` has a space, the pattern requires exactly one. The existing
  `(?<![a-z0-9])` / `(?![a-z0-9])` lookarounds stay and are what force the match
  to start at a token start and end at a token end.
- **What must match** (all three are BL-8's, and the first two are the failures
  R1002 names): `toma hawk` → `tomahawk` → MAG Tomahawk; `aor us master` →
  `aorus master` → Aorus Master; `pro r s` → the table's `pro rs` → Pro RS,
  which is the same rule making a hand-listed split form redundant.
- **What must NOT match, and this is the trap BL-8 names**: `b650` inside
  `theb650`; `b650` inside `b650e` or `xb650`; an alias against a run that
  starts or ends mid-token. A single fused token can only match a form that
  equals the whole token.
- **A space in the form is mandatory.** `steel legend` does not match a fused
  `steellegend`: R1002 requires every alias space to land on a token boundary,
  and a fused token has none there. Captions split words; they do not weld them.
- **Joining spans one space only** — `?` on a single space character, never
  `\s*` and never a character class that includes other punctuation. `b650/b850`
  yields two separate matches and never a fused one.
- **Precedence is unchanged**: forms still sort longest-normalized-first with
  file order preserved among equals, so `x670e` still beats `670e` at the same
  start position, and the pattern is byte-identical for a given table (R23).
  Length is measured on the normalized form, as today, not on the expanded
  pattern.
- **`matched_form` stays the text that matched** — `match.group(0)`, so the
  recall report shows `toma hawk` rather than `tomahawk`. This is what makes the
  fix inspectable in the artifact the owner already reads, and is the
  measurement OD-6 points at.
- **Empty and single-character forms behave as they do now**: an empty
  normalized form is skipped, and a one-character form has no interior position
  to make optional.
- **`docs/architecture.md`'s description of the matching rule is now
  incomplete** and is corrected in this slice: the alias pattern matches whole
  token runs, not literal strings, and the hyphen now folds — citing OD-6 and
  R1002. The passage explaining why the boundary lookarounds exist stays; it is
  still true and now carries more weight.

## Slice 3 — Recall is a number the suite reports, not a claim

- **Delivers:** a variant fixture of at least 52 mangled spellings, each with
  the canonical it must resolve to, run through `load_aliases` →
  `compile_matcher` → `find_mentions` against the shipped table; every row
  matches, and the three BL-8 failures are among them by name. The mishearings a
  join cannot recover (`air us elite`) are in the table, so Aorus Elite stops
  being reachable only by a correct pronunciation. Covers R1002.
- **Files:** `data/aliases.toml`, `tests/fixtures/alias_variants.json`, `tests/test_alias_recall.py`
- **Estimate:** ~160 lines

### The table edit, exactly

The coder's half of this slice is `data/aliases.toml` and nothing else:

- `Aorus Master` gains `air us master`; `Aorus Elite` gains `air us elite` — the
  spelling BL-8 measured. No bare `air us` entry: with no family word behind it
  it would match any sentence about any other Aorus board.
- `Pro RS` drops `pro-rs`, which slice 1 made a duplicate of `pro rs`, and drops
  `pro r s`, which slice 2 made redundant. Neither removal may change a match:
  the fixture carries both spellings and asserts they still resolve.
- Nothing else. Widening the table on speculation is the maintenance cost OD-6
  rejected as the general fix; the corpus and `aliases --check` are what should
  drive the next rows.

### The fixture

`tests/fixtures/alias_variants.json` — a list of `{"variant": …, "canonical": …}`
objects, following the repository's existing fixture convention
(`tests/fixtures/channel_entries.json`). Its header states that the set is
**reconstructed** from BL-8's description, not the original 52 rows, which were
measured offline and never committed.

At least 52 rows, covering every class the shipped table and `normalize`'s
docstring already name:

- spaced part numbers — `x 670 e`, `x 6 70 e`, `b 850`, `7800 x 3 d`;
- hyphenated forms — `x-670-e`, `steel-legend`, `pro-rs`;
- caption-split words — **`toma hawk`**, **`aor us master`**, `tai chi`,
  `cross hair`, `steel legends`;
- spelled-out initials — `m a g tomahawk`, `r o g strix`, `m p g carbon`;
- mishearings — **`air us elite`**, `tomohawk`, `steal legend`, `stryx`,
  `orus master`, `ty chi`, `tough gaming`;
- and the plain canonical spelling of every entry in the table, which is what
  makes the row count honest rather than padded with variants of one entity.

### Assertions

- Every row resolves to its stated canonical, through the real
  `load_aliases` → `compile_matcher` path against the shipped table, matching a
  synthesized one-cue transcript so `find_mentions` is what is exercised. A row
  that fails names the variant and the canonical it expected.
- The three named failures are asserted **individually as well**, by name, so a
  regression on exactly BL-8's evidence reads as its own failing test rather
  than as one row of a table.
- The negative cases from slice 2 are asserted here too, against the real table:
  `theb650` yields no mention, and `b650` yields none inside `b650e`.
- Every row's canonical exists in the table, so a typo in the fixture fails
  loudly rather than asserting nothing.
- The set is at least 52 rows — asserted, because "the measured set" is the
  thing R1002 asks to land and a fixture quietly shrinking to five rows would
  still pass every other assertion here.

## Out of scope

- **The ITX chipset variant (OD-7 / R1003).** `b850` inside `b850i` is a
  different decision with its own requirement and its own plan. It edits
  `compile_matcher` and probably `data/aliases.toml`, so **that plan and this
  one must not be built in parallel**; whichever lands second rebases onto the
  first. R1002's rule does not deliver it: `b850i` is one fused token, and this
  plan's whole guarantee is that a form never matches a proper substring of one.
- **The video description as a match target (OD-8 / R1004).** A better matcher
  over the fields already read; a new field is that decision's.
- **Matching across a cue boundary.** `find_mentions` normalizes and scans one
  cue at a time, so a name the cue break splits stays invisible. R1002 is about
  tokens within the text handed to the matcher. Worth a `BL-<n>` if the recall
  report shows it costing anything; not worth inventing a decision for here.
- **Where the alias table lives (OD-11 / R1007).** Slice 3 edits
  `data/aliases.toml` at the path the code reads today. The move to a tracked
  root path is that decision's plan, and this one is written so the edit is
  content-only — no loader, no config, no path.
- **Fuzzy or phonetic matching.** Mishearings stay table data by R1002's own
  words. Nothing here computes an edit distance.
- **Re-tuning the mention threshold (R4).** Recovered mentions raise counts and
  therefore admit videos; whether `N=3` is still right is a question for the
  in/out numbers this change moves, not a change to make alongside it.
